# Read by Cloudflare Pages and Netlify. Vercel needs `headers` in vercel.json.
#
# ⚠️ Two unrelated rules live here. Only the first one is temporary. ⚠️

# ── TEMPORARY: remove this block before launching on the real domain ──────
#
# This blocks ALL search-engine indexing, on every domain the site is served
# from — including a custom domain. It exists because the site currently lives
# on a throwaway *.pages.dev preview URL, and a preview URL that gets indexed
# will compete with the real domain in search results later.
#
# `X-Robots-Tag` is used rather than a robots.txt `Disallow`, because Disallow
# only stops crawling — Google can still index a URL it never crawled, from
# inbound links alone. The header is what actually keeps it out of the index.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# ── PERMANENT: keep this block ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The service worker is the one file that must never be served stale. Every
# asset it precaches is content-hashed, so sw.js is the only thing whose URL
# stays constant across deploys — if a CDN holds an old copy, installed users
# keep the old precache manifest and never see the new build.
#
# Browsers already cap service-worker script caching at 24h on their own, but
# that is a ceiling, not a fix: it still means up to a day of an installed app
# serving the previous release. The registration in components/PWAProvider.tsx
# polls hourly for exactly this reason, and the poll is pointless if the
# response it revalidates against is cached.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
